From: wsclean-mx <wsclean-mx@localhost>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 22:16:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] Metal gridding: evaluate ducc0's fitted polynomial (poly
 / poly-h variants)

ducc0's w-gridder does not evaluate exp(beta*((1-v^2)^e0 - 1)) at gridding time:
it approximates it with W piecewise polynomials of degree D and evaluates those.
This adds the variant that does the same, so the Metal kernel can be numerically
equivalent to the gridder it would replace.

* poly_sample implements ducc0's convention (normalise by the support, interval
  selection, local coordinate in [-1,1], Horner in the interval's monomials).
* grid_accumulate_poly evaluates 36 samples per visibility; the cached
  coefficients arrive as a buffer, and there is no per-visibility normalisation
  because ducc0 divides the kernel out of the image with a correction function.
* grid_accumulate_poly_hoisted keeps the 6+6 1D samples in registers.

Measured (min of 5, three consecutive runs agreeing): poly 5.64 ms (35 Mvis/s)
with rel_max 1.5e-7 against ducc0's convention - the best agreement of any
variant, since a Horner chain has no beta-amplified float32 error; poly-h
4.23 ms (47 Mvis/s, 1.3x faster than poly, rel_max 5.0e-6). Both slower than the
analytic form (2.33 ms), because 36 Horner chains cost more than 12 fast-math
pow/exp evaluations on this GPU. poly-h is the wiring candidate.

The probe gains --kernel-coeffs and reads the row's beta_norm/e0 from the file
rather than hard-coding them, so the analytic reference always describes the
same kernel as the coefficients.

